WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.148
Parametric equations give both coordinates in terms of a shared parameter

00:00:05.148 --> 00:00:10.760
Let the horizontal coordinate equal t and the vertical coordinate equal t squared

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:17.667
As t varies, these paired outputs trace the familiar parabola while retaining an order of travel.

00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:22.322
Evaluate both rules at the same parameter value

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:29.032
Negative two gives negative two, four; zero gives the origin; and positive two gives two, four

00:00:29.032 --> 00:00:37.000
Mixing coordinates from different parameter values would create a point that the motion never actually visits at that time.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.695
A different pair of rules traces a circle

00:00:41.695 --> 00:00:46.786
Cosine and sine automatically satisfy x squared plus y squared equals one

00:00:46.786 --> 00:00:54.000
As the parameter increases from zero to two pi, the point travels counterclockwise through one complete turn.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:01.993
Doubling the parameter inside both functions traces the same geometric circle twice as quickly

00:01:01.993 --> 00:01:07.729
Eliminating the parameter reveals the circle equation but loses this timing information

00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:14.900
A curve can therefore have many parametrizations with different directions, speeds, or numbers of traversals.
